 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 5.05 — Principal Bundles and Connections\n\n4.02 built a connection to compare tangent vectors at different points. That was one instance of a much more general construction, and this notebook gives the general one.\n\n**Principal bundles.** A principal $G$-bundle is a space $P$ with a free, transitive-on-fibres right action of $G$, and a projection $\\pi: P \\to M$. The fibre over each point is a copy of $G$, but with **no preferred identity** — there is no canonical way to say which element of the fibre is \"the\" identity, and that is the entire content of the theory.\n\nThe motivating example is the **frame bundle**: $P = \\{$orthonormal frames at points of $M\\}$, with $G = SO(n)$ acting by rotating frames. A point of $M$ has many frames and no canonical one.\n\n**Connections.** A connection is a $\\mathfrak g$-valued 1-form $A$ on $P$ — equivalently, in a local trivialisation, a $\\mathfrak g$-valued 1-form on $U \\subseteq M$:\n\n$$A = A_\\mu\\,dx^\\mu, \\qquad A_\\mu \\in \\mathfrak g.$$\n\nIt says which infinitesimal displacements in $P$ count as *horizontal*, hence how to transport along a curve. 4.02's Christoffel symbols are the components of this form for the frame bundle.\n\n**Curvature is the field strength.**\n\n$$F = dA + A\\wedge A, \\qquad F_{\\mu\\nu} = \\partial_\\mu A_\\nu - \\partial_\\nu A_\\mu + [A_\\mu, A_\\nu].$$\n\nThe bracket term is what 4.04 called the failure of covariant derivatives to commute — now visible as a genuine wedge product of algebra-valued forms.\n\n**Gauge transformations.** Changing the local trivialisation by $g: U \\to G$ acts by\n\n$$A \\mapsto gAg^{-1} - (dg)g^{-1}, \\qquad F \\mapsto gFg^{-1}.$$\n\n$A$ transforms *inhomogeneously* — it is not a tensor, exactly as $\\Gamma^k_{ij}$ was not (4.02). $F$ transforms by conjugation, so it is. **This is the same distinction, one level up.**\n\n**The Bianchi identity** becomes $DF = dF + [A,F] = 0$, or in components\n\n$$D_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu} + D_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda} + D_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu} = 0.$$\n\n**Two specialisations, one construction.**\n\n| $G$ | $P$ | $A$ | $F$ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $SO(n)$ | frame bundle | Levi-Civita connection | Riemann tensor |\n| $U(1)$ | circle bundle | electromagnetic potential | electromagnetic field |\n| $SU(3){\\times}SU(2){\\times}U(1)$ | — | gauge fields | the Standard Model |\n\nFor $G = U(1)$ the algebra is abelian, the bracket drops out, and $F_{\\mu\\nu} = \\partial_\\mu A_\\nu - \\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$ **is Maxwell's field tensor**, with the Bianchi identity $dF = 0$ being two of Maxwell's four equations. General relativity and electromagnetism are the same construction with different $G$.\n\nMaxima has no differential forms, so this notebook builds a small toolkit: $\\mathfrak g$-valued 1-forms as lists of matrices $[A_1,\\dots,A_n]$, and 2-forms as antisymmetric arrays of matrices.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\nbr(X, Y) := ratsimp( X.Y - Y.X )$\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------\n   A minimal toolkit for Lie-algebra-valued forms.\n     a g-valued 1-form  A = A_μ dx^μ   is the list [A_1, ..., A_n]\n     a g-valued 2-form  F              is the nested list F[μ][ν],\n                                       antisymmetric, of matrices\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------ */\ndA(A, xs) := block([n_], n_ : length(xs),\n  makelist( makelist(\n    ratsimp( diff(A[nu], xs[mu]) - diff(A[mu], xs[nu]) ),\n    nu, 1, n_), mu, 1, n_) )$\n\n/* the wedge A∧A of a g-valued 1-form with itself, in components */\nAwA(A, xs) := block([n_], n_ : length(xs),\n  makelist( makelist( br(A[mu], A[nu]), nu, 1, n_), mu, 1, n_) )$\n\nFstrength(A, xs) := block([D_, W_, n_],\n  D_ : dA(A, xs), W_ : AwA(A, xs), n_ : length(xs),\n  makelist( makelist( ratsimp(D_[mu][nu] + W_[mu][nu]), nu,1,n_), mu,1,n_) )$\n\n/* covariant exterior derivative: D_λ F_μν = ∂_λ F_μν + [A_λ, F_μν] */\ncovD(A, F, lam, mu, nu, xs) :=\n  ratsimp( diff(F[mu][nu], xs[lam]) + br(A[lam], F[mu][nu]) )$\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------\n   G = U(1):  the algebra is iℝ, so all brackets vanish.\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------ */\nxs4 : [tt, x, y, z]$\ndepends([a0, a1, a2, a3], xs4)$\nAu1 : [ %i*a0, %i*a1, %i*a2, %i*a3 ]$      /* u(1)-valued: 1×1, i.e. scalars */\nFu1 : makelist( makelist(\n        ratsimp( diff(Au1[nu], xs4[mu]) - diff(Au1[mu], xs4[nu]) ),\n        nu,1,4), mu,1,4)$\n\nprint(\"G = U(1).  A = i(a₀,a₁,a₂,a₃) dx^μ, brackets all zero.\")$\nprint(\"   F₀₁ = ∂₀A₁ − ∂₁A₀ = \", Fu1[1][2])$\nprint(\"      i(∂_t a₁ − ∂_x a₀) — up to the i, an electric field component\")$\nprint(\"   F₁₂ = ∂₁A₂ − ∂₂A₁ = \", Fu1[2][3])$\nprint(\"      i(∂_x a₂ − ∂_y a₁) — a magnetic field component\")$\nprint(\"   antisymmetry F₀₁ + F₁₀ = \", ratsimp(Fu1[1][2] + Fu1[2][1]))$\n\n/* Bianchi dF = 0 in the abelian case */\nbad : 0$\nfor l:1 thru 4 do for m:1 thru 4 do for n:1 thru 4 do\n  if ratsimp( diff(Fu1[m][n], xs4[l]) + diff(Fu1[n][l], xs4[m])\n            + diff(Fu1[l][m], xs4[n]) ) # 0 then bad : bad + 1$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   Bianchi ∂_λF_μν + ∂_μF_νλ + ∂_νF_λμ = 0 over all 64 triples:\")$\nprint(\"      violations = \", bad)$\nprint(\"   The four independent components of dF = 0 are ∇·B = 0 and\")$\nprint(\"   ∇×E + ∂B/∂t = 0 — the two SOURCELESS Maxwell equations.  They\")$\nprint(\"   hold for any A whatsoever, because they are an identity about\")$\nprint(\"   d², not a law of physics.  The other two, d⋆F = J, are dynamics\")$\nprint(\"   and need a metric to define ⋆.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "G = U(1).  A = i(a₀,a₁,a₂,a₃) dx^μ, brackets all zero.\n   F₀₁ = ∂₀A₁ − ∂₁A₀ =  %i*'diff(a1,tt,1)-%i*'diff(a0,x,1)\n      i(∂_t a₁ − ∂_x a₀) — up to the i, an electric field component\n   F₁₂ = ∂₁A₂ − ∂₂A₁ =  %i*'diff(a2,x,1)-%i*'diff(a1,y,1)\n      i(∂_x a₂ − ∂_y a₁) — a magnetic field component\n   antisymmetry F₀₁ + F₁₀ =  0\n   Bianchi ∂_λF_μν + ∂_μF_νλ + ∂_νF_λμ = 0 over all 64 triples:\n      violations =  0\n   The four independent components of dF = 0 are ∇·B = 0 and\n   ∇×E + ∂B/∂t = 0 — the two SOURCELESS Maxwell equations.  They\n   hold for any A whatsoever, because they are an identity about\n   d², not a law of physics.  The other two, d⋆F = J, are dynamics\n   and need a metric to define ⋆."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* ------------------------------------------------------------------\n   G = SU(2): a genuinely non-abelian gauge field on ℝ³\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------ */\nxs3 : [x, y, z]$\nEb : [ matrix([0,-%i/2],[-%i/2,0]),\n       matrix([0,-1/2],[1/2,0]),\n       matrix([-%i/2,0],[0,%i/2]) ]$\n\n/* nine independent component functions a^k_μ(x,y,z) */\nasym : makelist( makelist( concat(aa, mu, k), k, 1, 3), mu, 1, 3)$\napply(depends, [flatten(asym), xs3])$\nAsu : makelist( sum( asym[mu][k]*Eb[k], k, 1, 3), mu, 1, 3)$\n\nFsu : Fstrength(Asu, xs3)$\nprint(\"SU(2) on ℝ³, A_μ = Σₖ a^k_μ(x,y,z) Eₖ :\")$\nprint(\"   A₁ = \", Asu[1])$\nprint(\"   F₁₂ is anti-Hermitian and traceless (i.e. in su(2)) ? \",\n      is( ratsimp( Fsu[1][2] + conjugate(transpose(Fsu[1][2])) )\n          = zeromatrix(2,2)\n          and ratsimp(mat_trace(Fsu[1][2])) = 0 ))$\nprint(\"   F antisymmetric ?  F₁₂ + F₂₁ = \", ratsimp(Fsu[1][2] + Fsu[2][1]))$\n\n/* --- the non-abelian Bianchi identity --- */\nbad2 : 0$\nfor l:1 thru 3 do for m:1 thru 3 do for n:1 thru 3 do\n  if ratsimp( covD(Asu,Fsu,l,m,n,xs3) + covD(Asu,Fsu,m,n,l,xs3)\n            + covD(Asu,Fsu,n,l,m,xs3) ) # zeromatrix(2,2)\n    then bad2 : bad2 + 1$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   Bianchi  D_λF_μν + D_μF_νλ + D_νF_λμ = 0  over all 27 triples:\")$\nprint(\"      violations = \", bad2)$\nprint(\"   The ordinary derivative alone does NOT satisfy this — the\")$\nprint(\"   [A_λ, F_μν] terms are needed.  Check that:\")$\nbad3 : 0$\nfor l:1 thru 3 do for m:1 thru 3 do for n:1 thru 3 do\n  if ratsimp( diff(Fsu[m][n],xs3[l]) + diff(Fsu[n][l],xs3[m])\n            + diff(Fsu[l][m],xs3[n]) ) # zeromatrix(2,2)\n    then bad3 : bad3 + 1$\nprint(\"      violations of the NAIVE ∂-only version = \", bad3, \" out of 27\")$\nprint(\"   So the covariant derivative is not decoration.  In the abelian\")$\nprint(\"   case above the two versions coincide, which is why Maxwell needs\")$\nprint(\"   no such correction.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 3,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "SU(2) on ℝ³, A_μ = Σₖ a^k_μ(x,y,z) Eₖ :\n   A₁ =\n        matrix([-((%i*aa13)/2),-(aa12/2)-(%i*aa11)/2],\n               [aa12/2-(%i*aa11)/2,(%i*aa13)/2])\n   F₁₂ is anti-Hermitian and traceless (i.e. in su(2)) ?  true\n   F antisymmetric ?  F₁₂ + F₂₁ =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])\n   Bianchi  D_λF_μν + D_μF_νλ + D_νF_λμ = 0  over all 27 triples:\n      violations =  0\n   The ordinary derivative alone does NOT satisfy this — the\n   [A_λ, F_μν] terms are needed.  Check that:\n      violations of the NAIVE ∂-only version =  6  out of 27\n   So the covariant derivative is not decoration.  In the abelian\n   case above the two versions coincide, which is why Maxwell needs\n   no such correction."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* ------------------------------------------------------------------\n   Gauge transformations:  A ↦ gAg⁻¹ − (dg)g⁻¹,   F ↦ gFg⁻¹\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------ */\ndepends(ph, xs3)$\ngg   : matrix([exp(-%i*ph/2), 0],[0, exp(%i*ph/2)])$   /* = exp(φ E₃) */\nggi  : matrix([exp(%i*ph/2), 0],[0, exp(-%i*ph/2)])$\nprint(\"g = exp(φ E₃) = \", gg, \"   g g⁻¹ = \", ratsimp(gg . ggi))$\n\ngauge(A, gg_, ggi_, xs) := makelist(\n  ratsimp( gg_ . A[mu] . ggi_ - diff(gg_, xs[mu]) . ggi_ ), mu, 1, length(xs))$\n\nAp : gauge(Asu, gg, ggi, xs3)$\nFp : Fstrength(Ap, xs3)$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   A′₁ still in su(2) ?  A′₁ + A′₁† = \",\n      ratsimp( Ap[1] + conjugate(transpose(Ap[1])) ),\n      \"   tr = \", ratsimp(mat_trace(Ap[1])))$\n\nbadg : 0$\nfor m:1 thru 3 do for n:1 thru 3 do\n  if ratsimp( Fp[m][n] - gg . Fsu[m][n] . ggi ) # zeromatrix(2,2)\n    then badg : badg + 1$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   F′_μν − g F_μν g⁻¹  over all 9 components:  violations = \", badg)$\nprint(\"   F transforms by CONJUGATION: it is a tensor (an ad-valued 2-form).\")$\n\n/* A itself does not */\nbadA : 0$\nfor m:1 thru 3 do\n  if ratsimp( Ap[m] - gg . Asu[m] . ggi ) # zeromatrix(2,2)\n    then badA : badA + 1$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   A′_μ − g A_μ g⁻¹  (i.e. dropping the −(dg)g⁻¹ term): \",\n      badA, \" of 3 components differ\")$\nprint(\"   The extra term is \", ratsimp( -diff(gg, xs3[1]) . ggi ),\n      \"  for μ = 1.\")$\nprint(\"   A is NOT a tensor.  Exactly the status of Γᵏᵢⱼ in 4.02, and for\")$\nprint(\"   exactly the same reason: it encodes a choice of trivialisation,\")$\nprint(\"   there a choice of coordinates.\")$\n\n/* --- pure gauge: A = −(dg)g⁻¹ has F = 0 --- */\nAzero : [ zeromatrix(2,2), zeromatrix(2,2), zeromatrix(2,2) ]$\nApure : gauge(Azero, gg, ggi, xs3)$\nFpure : Fstrength(Apure, xs3)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"PURE GAUGE.  Starting from A = 0 and transforming by g:\")$\nprint(\"   A^pure₁ = \", Apure[1], \"   ≠ 0\")$\nprint(\"   F^pure  = \", ratsimp(Fpure[1][2]), \", \", ratsimp(Fpure[1][3]),\n      \", \", ratsimp(Fpure[2][3]))$\nprint(\"   A non-zero connection with zero curvature.  This is 4.04's polar-\")$\nprint(\"   coordinate plane in gauge-theoretic dress: Γ ≠ 0 with R = 0, the\")$\nprint(\"   connection reporting a choice rather than a geometry.\")$\nprint(\"   On a space with non-trivial π₁ a flat connection can still have\")$\nprint(\"   non-trivial holonomy — that is the Aharonov–Bohm effect, and 4.02\")$\nprint(\"   met the Riemannian version of it.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 4,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "g = exp(φ E₃) =  matrix([%e^-((%i*ph)/2),0],[0,%e^((%i*ph)/2)])    g g⁻¹ =\n                matrix([1,0],[0,1])\n   A′₁ still in su(2) ?  A′₁ + A′₁† =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])    tr =  0\n   F′_μν − g F_μν g⁻¹  over all 9 components:  violations =  0\n   F transforms by CONJUGATION: it is a tensor (an ad-valued 2-form).\n   A′_μ − g A_μ g⁻¹  (i.e. dropping the −(dg)g⁻¹ term):\n  3  of 3 components differ\n   The extra term is\n                     matrix([(%i*'diff(ph,x,1))/2,0],\n                            [0,-((%i*'diff(ph,x,1))/2)])   for μ = 1.\n   A is NOT a tensor.  Exactly the status of Γᵏᵢⱼ in 4.02, and for\n   exactly the same reason: it encodes a choice of trivialisation,\n   there a choice of coordinates.\nPURE GAUGE.  Starting from A = 0 and transforming by g:\n   A^pure₁ =  matrix([(%i*'diff(ph,x,1))/2,0],[0,-((%i*'diff(ph,x,1))/2)])\n                ≠ 0\n   F^pure  =  matrix([0,0],[0,0]) ,  matrix([0,0],[0,0]) ,\n             matrix([0,0],[0,0])\n   A non-zero connection with zero curvature.  This is 4.04's polar-\n   coordinate plane in gauge-theoretic dress: Γ ≠ 0 with R = 0, the\n   connection reporting a choice rather than a geometry.\n   On a space with non-trivial π₁ a flat connection can still have\n   non-trivial holonomy — that is the Aharonov–Bohm effect, and 4.02\n   met the Riemannian version of it."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* ------------------------------------------------------------------\n   The Levi-Civita connection as an SO(2) gauge field on a surface.\n   Forms in 2 dimensions:  a 1-form is [a₁,a₂] w.r.t. (dx¹,dx²);\n   a 2-form is the single coefficient of dx¹∧dx².\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------ */\nd1(al, xs) := ratsimp( diff(al[2], xs[1]) - diff(al[1], xs[2]) )$\nwed(al, be) := ratsimp( al[1]*be[2] - al[2]*be[1] )$\n\n/* Cartan's first structure equation, solved for the connection 1-form:\n      dθ¹ = ω¹₂ ∧ θ²,   dθ² = −ω¹₂ ∧ θ¹   (ω²₁ = −ω¹₂ since so(2) is skew)\n   Two linear equations for the two components of ω. */\nconnform(th1, th2, xs) := block([w1, w2, sol],\n  sol : linsolve( [ wed([w1,w2], th2) = d1(th1, xs),\n                   -wed([w1,w2], th1) = d1(th2, xs) ], [w1, w2] ),\n  [ ratsimp(rhs(sol[1])), ratsimp(rhs(sol[2])) ] )$\n\n/* second structure equation: dω¹₂ = −K θ¹∧θ² */\ncurv_from_frame(th1, th2, xs) := block([om],\n  om : connform(th1, th2, xs),\n  ratsimp( - d1(om, xs) / wed(th1, th2) ) )$\n\nframe_report(th1, th2, xs, label) := block([om],\n  om : connform(th1, th2, xs),\n  print(\"   \", label),\n  print(\"      θ¹ = \", th1, \"   θ² = \", th2),\n  print(\"      ω¹₂ = \", om, \"     dω¹₂ = \", d1(om, xs)),\n  print(\"      K = −dω¹₂ / (θ¹∧θ²) = \", curv_from_frame(th1, th2, xs)) )$\n\nassume(R0 > 0, u > 0, u < %pi, y > 0, rr > 0)$\nprint(\"CARTAN'S STRUCTURE EQUATIONS on surfaces.\")$\nprint(\"so(2) is 1-dimensional and ABELIAN, so the A∧A term vanishes and\")$\nprint(\"the curvature 2-form is simply F = dω — the same situation as\")$\nprint(\"electromagnetism, with SO(2) ≅ U(1) in place of the gauge group.\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nframe_report([R0, 0], [0, R0*sin(u)], [u, v], \"sphere of radius R₀\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nframe_report([1/y, 0], [0, 1/y], [x, y], \"hyperbolic half-plane\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nframe_report([1, 0], [0, rr], [rr, th], \"Euclidean plane, polar coordinates\")$\n\n/* the general surface of revolution */\nprint(\"\")$\ndepends(f, u)$\nframe_report([1, 0], [0, f], [u, v], \"surface of revolution, θ² = f(u) dv\")$\nprint(\"      which is −f''/f, the formula 3.04 derived from the second\")$\nprint(\"      fundamental form and 4.06 used for Jacobi fields.\")$\nremove(f, dependency)$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Every value agrees with 4.04, which computed them from Christoffel\")$\nprint(\"symbols and a four-index Riemann tensor.  Here they come from two\")$\nprint(\"structure equations and a 2×2 linear solve — the frame-bundle route\")$\nprint(\"is simply less work, and it is the route that generalises to gauge\")$\nprint(\"groups other than SO(n).\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"The dictionary:\")$\nprint(\"   ω¹₂        the connection A, an so(2)-valued 1-form\")$\nprint(\"   dω¹₂       the curvature F = dA (no A∧A: so(2) is abelian)\")$\nprint(\"   K          the single independent component of F, since in 2D\")$\nprint(\"              both Λ²T*M and so(2) are 1-dimensional\")$\nprint(\"   frames     the fibre: SO(2) worth of orthonormal frames per point,\")$\nprint(\"              with no canonical one — which is the whole reason a\")$\nprint(\"              CONNECTION is needed to compare them.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 5,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "CARTAN'S STRUCTURE EQUATIONS on surfaces.\nso(2) is 1-dimensional and ABELIAN, so the A∧A term vanishes and\nthe curvature 2-form is simply F = dω — the same situation as\nelectromagnetism, with SO(2) ≅ U(1) in place of the gauge group.\n    sphere of radius R₀\n      θ¹ =  [R0,0]    θ² =  [0,R0*sin(u)]\n      ω¹₂ =  [0,cos(u)]      dω¹₂ =  -sin(u)\n      K = −dω¹₂ / (θ¹∧θ²) =  1/R0^2\n    hyperbolic half-plane\n      θ¹ =  [1/y,0]    θ² =  [0,1/y]\n      ω¹₂ =  [1/y,0]      dω¹₂ =  1/y^2\n      K = −dω¹₂ / (θ¹∧θ²) =  -1\n    Euclidean plane, polar coordinates\n      θ¹ =  [1,0]    θ² =  [0,rr]\n      ω¹₂ =  [0,1]      dω¹₂ =  0\n      K = −dω¹₂ / (θ¹∧θ²) =  0\n    surface of revolution, θ² = f(u) dv\n      θ¹ =  [1,0]    θ² =  [0,f]\n      ω¹₂ =  [0,'diff(f,u,1)]      dω¹₂ =  'diff(f,u,2)\n      K = −dω¹₂ / (θ¹∧θ²) =  -('diff(f,u,2)/f)\n      which is −f''/f, the formula 3.04 derived from the second\n      fundamental form and 4.06 used for Jacobi fields.\nEvery value agrees with 4.04, which computed them from Christoffel\nsymbols and a four-index Riemann tensor.  Here they come from two\nstructure equations and a 2×2 linear solve — the frame-bundle route\nis simply less work, and it is the route that generalises to gauge\ngroups other than SO(n).\nThe dictionary:\n   ω¹₂        the connection A, an so(2)-valued 1-form\n   dω¹₂       the curvature F = dA (no A∧A: so(2) is abelian)\n   K          the single independent component of F, since in 2D\n              both Λ²T*M and so(2) are 1-dimensional\n   frames     the fibre: SO(2) worth of orthonormal frames per point,\n              with no canonical one — which is the whole reason a\n              CONNECTION is needed to compare them."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n$$F = dA + A\\wedge A, \\qquad A \\mapsto gAg^{-1} - (dg)g^{-1}, \\qquad F \\mapsto gFg^{-1}$$\n\n**$U(1)$ gives Maxwell for free.** With an abelian algebra the bracket drops out and $F_{\\mu\\nu} = \\partial_\\mu A_\\nu - \\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$. The Bianchi identity was checked over all 64 index triples on $\\mathbb{R}^{1,3}$: **zero violations**, for a completely arbitrary $A$. Those four independent components are $\\nabla\\cdot B = 0$ and $\\nabla\\times E + \\partial_tB = 0$ — half of Maxwell's equations, holding as an identity about $d^2$ rather than as a physical law. The other half, $d\\star F = J$, is dynamics and needs a metric to define $\\star$.\n\n**Non-abelian: the covariant derivative earns its keep.** For a generic $SU(2)$ connection on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ with nine independent component functions:\n\n| identity | violations (of 27 triples) |\n|---|---|\n| $D_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu} + D_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda} + D_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu} = 0$ | **0** |\n| the naive $\\partial$-only version | **6** |\n\nThe $[A_\\lambda, F_{\\mu\\nu}]$ terms are not decoration. In the abelian case the two versions coincide, which is exactly why Maxwell needs no correction.\n\n**$A$ is not a tensor; $F$ is.** Under $g = \\exp(\\varphi E_3)$ with $\\varphi$ an arbitrary function, $F'_{\\mu\\nu} - gF_{\\mu\\nu}g^{-1}$ vanished in all nine components, while $A'_\\mu - gA_\\mu g^{-1}$ differed in **all three** — by exactly the inhomogeneous term $-(\\partial_\\mu g)g^{-1}$. This is the status of $\\Gamma^k_{ij}$ in 4.02, one level of abstraction up, and for the same reason: $A$ records a choice of trivialisation, $\\Gamma$ a choice of coordinates.\n\n**Pure gauge.** Transforming $A = 0$ by $g$ gives a non-zero connection with $F$ identically zero in every component. That is 4.04's polar-coordinate plane in gauge-theoretic dress — $\\Gamma \\ne 0$ with $R \\equiv 0$, the connection reporting a choice rather than a geometry. On a space with non-trivial $\\pi_1$ a flat connection can still carry non-trivial holonomy, which is the Aharonov–Bohm effect.\n\n**The Levi-Civita connection is an $SO(2)$ gauge field.** Cartan's structure equations $d\\theta^1 = \\omega^1_{\\;2}\\wedge\\theta^2$, $d\\theta^2 = -\\omega^1_{\\;2}\\wedge\\theta^1$, $d\\omega^1_{\\;2} = -K\\,\\theta^1\\wedge\\theta^2$ were solved as a $2\\times2$ linear system:\n\n| surface | $\\omega^1_{\\;2}$ | $K$ |\n|---|---|---|\n| sphere of radius $R_0$ | $\\cos u\\,dv$ | $1/R_0^2$ |\n| hyperbolic half-plane | $dx/y$ | $-1$ |\n| plane in polar coordinates | $d\\theta$ | $0$ |\n| revolution, $\\theta^2 = f\\,dv$ | $f'\\,dv$ | $-f''/f$ |\n\nEvery value matches 4.04, which reached them through Christoffel symbols and a four-index Riemann tensor. The frame route is two structure equations and a linear solve, and unlike the Christoffel route it generalises to gauge groups other than $SO(n)$.\n\nNote what makes the surface case easy: $\\mathfrak{so}(2)$ is **abelian**, so $A\\wedge A = 0$ and $F = d\\omega$ exactly — a surface is, gauge-theoretically, an electromagnetism problem.\n\n**One construction, several subjects.**\n\n| $G$ | $A$ | $F$ |\n|---|---|---|\n| $SO(n)$ | Levi-Civita connection | Riemann tensor |\n| $U(1)$ | electromagnetic potential | electromagnetic field |\n| $SU(2)$, $SU(3)$ | Yang–Mills fields | field strengths |\n\n## Next\n\n**5.06 — Characteristic Classes** asks what survives when the gauge is forgotten. Invariant polynomials in $F$ — $\\operatorname{tr}(F)$, $\\operatorname{tr}(F\\wedge F)$, $\\det(I + F)$ — are gauge-invariant by construction and closed by Bianchi, so they define de Rham classes independent of the connection. Chern–Weil theory turns curvature into topology, and Gauss–Bonnet (3.08) becomes the two-dimensional case of a general theorem.\n\n## References\n\n- Kobayashi, S. and Nomizu, K. (1963, 1969) *Foundations of Differential Geometry*, vols I–II. New York: Wiley. The reference account of principal bundles, connections and holonomy. Volume I Chapter II is where the definitions used here are pinned down.\n- Nakahara, M. (2003) *Geometry, Topology and Physics*. 2nd edn. Bristol: Institute of Physics. Chapters 9–10. Written for physicists, and the clearest place to see the gauge-theory and Riemannian pictures written in the same notation.\n- Baez, J.C. and Muniain, J.P. (1994) *Gauge Fields, Knots and Gravity*. Singapore: World Scientific. Part II builds exactly the $U(1)$-to-$SU(2)$ progression of this notebook, from Maxwell up, with unusual care about what is definition and what is theorem.\n- Frankel, T. (2011) *The Geometry of Physics: An Introduction*. 3rd edn. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. Chapters 16–18, and the best treatment of Cartan's structure equations at the level used here.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9). Chapter 4 problems develop the frame formulation of the Levi-Civita connection as an alternative to the Christoffel computation.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed. Maxima has no differential forms, so the toolkit in the first cell — $\\mathfrak g$-valued 1-forms as lists of matrices, 2-forms as antisymmetric nested lists, and a separate 2-dimensional wedge for the frame computation — is hand-rolled and its correctness is only as good as the identities it reproduces. That is why the Bianchi and gauge-covariance checks are run over every index combination rather than a sample.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
}